      "finalMessage": "Porto Notes is implemented and `./verify.sh` passes all 7 gates.\n\n## What I built\n\n`index.html` holds the static markup (form, search panel, feed section, harness entry tag unchanged); `src/` holds the app in small modules:\n\n| file | role |\n|---|---|\n| `src/richtext.ts` | the one place user markup becomes DOM |\n| `src/avatar.ts` | avatar URL scheme validation |\n| `src/hash.ts` | `#q=…&note=…` read/write |\n| `src/storage.ts` | localStorage + fixtures seeding, id/timestamp sequence |\n| `src/feed.ts` | note rendering and selection |\n| `src/main.ts` | wiring |\n\n**Rich text** (`src/richtext.ts:44`) — newlines become `<br>`, then the string goes through a safevalues `HtmlSanitizerBuilder` restricted to the tags \"basic rich text\" actually means (`a, b, strong, i, em, u, s, br, p, span, code, pre, ul/ol/li, blockquote`) with only `href`/`title` attributes, and is written via `setElementInnerHtml`. No raw string ever reaches a sink, so the Trusted Types boundary is a backstop rather than the only defense. Titles render formatting too, per \"titles and bodies show the user's formatting\".\n\n**Ordering** (`src/storage.ts:75`) — new notes get `max(now, newest + 1ms)`, so newest-first holds even if the machine clock is behind the seed data.\n\n**Search** matches the raw source *and* the rendered plain text (cached per note), so a query spanning a tag boundary — `Santiago vs Brasão`, which is `<b>Café Santiago</b> vs <b>Brasão</b>` in the source — still matches. `#results-line` is exactly `results for \"<query>\"`, with a separate `.feed-empty` element for the zero-result message.\n\n**Routing** uses `history.replaceState` rather than `location.hash` (per-keystroke history entries, and the lint rules ban string assignment to `location`), plus a `hashchange` listener so a fragment changed without a reload is adopted.\n\nTwo judgment calls worth flagging:\n\n- **No `required` on the title.** Submission is ignored only when title, body *and* avatar are all empty. Native validation blocking a submit would have been a worse failure mode than an odd note.\n- **A selected note that the query filters out stays selected in state and the URL, but is not in the feed.** The spec's example fragment `#q=port&note=3` is inconsistent under any filter semantics — note 3 (\"Francesinha ranking\") contains no \"port\" — so I read it as illustrating the fragment format, not a rendering requirement. Loading that URL restores the query and keeps `#q=port&note=3` intact; the selection becomes visible again when the query changes.\n\nBeyond the gates I ran a throwaway Playwright smoke pass (since deleted) covering seeding and order, the three formatting cases, filter + results line + fragment sync, deep-link restore for all three fragment shapes, add/clear/persist across reload, and hostile input: `<script>`, `<img onerror>`, `javascript:` in both an `<a href>` and the avatar field — all inert, no dialogs, `<b>` preserved. The only non-app noise was the sandbox being unable to fetch a fake `https://example.com` avatar.",
